rules:
  - id: cross-pollination-dedupe
    class: ask
    when: { write: "*cross-pollination*.md", new: true }
    dedupe-of: genesis/research/README.md
    why: "Check the Research Index (README.md) first — this topic may be catalogued (e.g. the hypha* name-collision triple). Extend the entry rather than starting a parallel note."
  - id: mint-pass-at-close
    class: inject
    when: { write: "*.md", new: true }
    dedupe-of: genesis/data/timeline/backlog/CLUSTERS.md
    why: >
      Research engagements close with a MINT PASS (operator decision 2026-08-04): each surviving
      take/adopt item folds as a ROW into the matching backlog cluster file (see
      genesis/data/timeline/backlog/CLUSTERS.md), citing this survey's epr: slug — never as a spray
      of standalone backlog entries. Takes not worth a cluster row die honestly in the survey's
      prose. The survey's Outputs section then points at the clusters it fed. This is the first hop
      of the provenance chain (research → cluster row → spec → code + scenario → chronicle).
  - id: sovereignty-frame-declared
    class: ask
    when: { write: "*.md", contains-any: ["sovereign", "Sovereign"], new: true }
    require-frontmatter: [sovereignty-frame]
    why: >
      The MEMBRANE rule (operator decision 2026-08-05). Surveys must quote, reason inside, and argue
      against the prevailing self-sovereignty ontology, because nearly every project we survey still
      uses it (Playnet's "autonomic identifiers", p2panda/SSB's crypto-identity lineage, the whole
      DID/VC/UCAN stack). Prompting on every quotation would tax the critique itself and push authors
      toward euphemism — the opposite of clarity. So research does not gate the VOCABULARY; it gates
      the DIFFERENTIATION. A survey that reaches for the words declares
      `sovereignty-frame: adversary|bounded|bridge-legibility` in its frontmatter and says plainly
      how the Elohim ontology differs: identity is imago dei — an inviolable right BACKSTOPPED by
      community and institutional expression, never a self-asserted crypto primitive; real agency
      comes from the community backstopping the individual. Naming the frame is what quarantines it
      at the boundary, so an examined borrowing inoculates instead of leaking. The same marker is the
      ONLY silencer for the global PostToolUse landing-logger, so one line does both jobs. Birth-time
      only — maintenance of an existing survey is never trapped. The membrane ends at this directory:
      canon (genesis/docs/content/elohim-protocol/) binds the unrelaxed sovereignty-ontology-guard
      policy, where an apex frame would become doctrine rather than a quotation.
  - id: readme-blind-reader-review
    class: measure
    when: { write: "README*.md" }
    measure:
      kind: level
    why: >
      Nearer-wins RELAXATION of the repo-root readme-blind-reader-review rule (operator decision
      2026-08-04): research/ is the messy desk of creativity — context leak between the author's head
      and the page is acceptable here, because these notes exist to help us solve problems, not to
      stand alone for external audiences. Blind-reader isolation stays mandatory for the
      external-facing surfaces (epics, manifestos, READMEs elsewhere in the repo); this override
      demotes it to observation-only for the Research Index and any research README.
---
# research — surveys + prior-art

The Research Index (`README.md`) is the canonical catalog of what has been surveyed, including the
name-collision scar tissue (`hypha-dao` ≠ `hyphacoop` ≠ `hypha-network`). A new `*cross-pollination*.md`
is checked against the index at birth so authors fold existing coverage rather than starting a parallel
note. Edits to existing surveys and all other research files are unaffected.

This directory is exempt from the repo-root README blind-reader review (relaxed to `measure` above):
research is the messy desk — surveys and the index may lean on shared context freely. Documents that
graduate out of research into the canon (epics, manifestos, module READMEs) pick the guard back up in
their destination directories.

## The sovereignty membrane (why this directory reads the guard differently)

Research is where we meet other people's ontologies in their own words. Almost every project worth
surveying still frames identity as *self-sovereignty* — so a survey has to say the phrase in order to
disagree with it, and the sovereignty-ontology-guard, tuned for surfaces where such framing would
become a design, mis-reads that disagreement as adoption. Relaxing it here to `measure` is a
**context boundary**, not a loosening of the conviction: the vocabulary is admitted so it can be
examined, and the price of admission is that the survey says plainly where we differ. Name the
adversary frame, quote it accurately, argue with it, and record the difference — an unexamined
borrowing is what leaks; an examined one is inoculation. Every doc that crosses back out of this
directory carries the unrelaxed rule with it.

## How research is written (the shape that composes)

The messy desk still has a shape, because downstream hops consume it: (1) **graded claims** — every
load-bearing claim carries ✅ verified-in-source / ◐ single-source / ⚠ web-only, so a cluster row or
spec citing the survey inherits an honest confidence; (2) **sorted verdicts** — take / study / leave /
watch (or green/red team), so the mint pass has a mechanical input; (3) **grounded adjudication** —
verdicts adjudicate against *current build-state* (file:line), never the durable spec; (4) **a method
note and credit** — who searched what, what the adversarial pass corrected. On close, the **mint pass**
(rule above) folds surviving takes into backlog cluster rows citing the survey's `epr:` slug — the
first hop of the provenance chain documented in `genesis/data/timeline/backlog/CLUSTERS.md`. A survey
whose takes were never minted is an idea sink with no drain; the survey's Outputs section names the
clusters it fed so the gap is visible at a glance.
